#: ../../source/docs/common-measuring-vibration.rst:5
#: b7ac4c27f9514e62b88285a5c7b734d1
msgid "Measuring Vibration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-measuring-vibration.rst:9
#: 87264848b2df452eb30d93298a05b362
msgid "Autopilots have accelerometers that are sensitive to vibrations. These accelerometer values are combined with barometer and GPS data to estimate the vehicle's position. With excessive vibrations, the estimate can be thrown off and lead to very bad performance in modes that rely on accurate positioning (e.g. on Copter: AltHold, Loiter, RTL, Guided, Position and Auto flight modes)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-measuring-vibration.rst:16
#: e1ec117bb34542da963f68e97015e1cb
msgid "These instructions explain how to measure the vibration levels. If you find they are out-of-tolerance then follow the advice found on the :ref:`Vibration Damping <common-vibration-damping>` page."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-measuring-vibration.rst:20
#: 0e004eb8485a45edba324d6322b07388
msgid "Real-Time view in Ground Station"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-measuring-vibration.rst:22
#: 82c3c58392634b829a4e5aab71fc5146
msgid "Ground Stations can display a real-time view of vibration and clipping.  If using the Mission Planner click on \"Vibe\" on the HUD to display the current vibration levels."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-measuring-vibration.rst:28
#: 44cf8d2cab844746be9efc5489878080
msgid "Vibration levels below 30m/s/s are normally acceptable.  Levels above 30m/s/s *may* have problems and levels above 60m/s/s nearly always have problems with position or altitude hold."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-measuring-vibration.rst:31
#: 0a95f725ad3540478e7ed83b7a8acd56
msgid "Vibe Dataflash Log message"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-measuring-vibration.rst:33
#: 1ce901a8e53249b5acbf62f5fcd1a38d
msgid "Check the recorded Vibe levels are mostly below 30m/s/s"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-measuring-vibration.rst:35
#: 35551b99643f4b1ca685427d90f4b57f
msgid "Perform a regular flight (i.e. not just a gentle hover) of at least a few minutes and :ref:`download the dataflash logs <common-downloading-and-analyzing-data-logs-in-mission-planner_downloading_logs_via_mavlink>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-measuring-vibration.rst:37
#: ab07b664f00e4be7b4306b3e194f95b5
msgid "Using the Mission Planner or other ground station graph the VIBE message's VibeX, VibeY and VibeZ values.  These show the standard deviation of the primary accelerometer's output in m/s/s.  The image below, taken from a 3DR IRIS, shows normal levels which are below 15m/s/s but occasionally peak to 30m/s/s.  Maximum acceptable values appear to be below 30m/s/s (see second picture below)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-measuring-vibration.rst:43
#: 2de9c0a49dc1432c8cd5a177de846de4
msgid "Graph the Clip0, Clip1 and Clip2 values which increase each time one of the accelerometers reaches its maximum limit (16G).  Ideally these should be zero for the whole flight but low numbers (<100) are likely ok especially if they occur during hard landings.  A regularly increasing number through the log indicates a serious vibration problem that :ref:`should be fixed <common-vibration-damping>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-measuring-vibration.rst:54
#: 16ffdcd19eb44353a931ee954869f90c
msgid "This is an example of a vehicle that had position estimate problems due to high vibrations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-measuring-vibration.rst:61
#: be8da001693542228f2699b85e6ee59e
msgid "The algorithm for calculating the vibration levels can be seen in the `AP_InertialSensor.cpp's calc_vibration_and_clipping() <https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/blob/master/libraries/AP_InertialSensor/AP_InertialSensor.cpp#L1609>`__ method but in short it involves calculating the standard deviation of the accelerometer readings like this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-measuring-vibration.rst:66
#: b68f5dea46ef4b45bfffde16200649cd
msgid "Capture the raw x, y and z accelerometer values from the primary IMU"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-measuring-vibration.rst:67
#: 330bbe27788b4555994fa0dda73d98bb
msgid "High-pass filter the raw values at 5hz to remove the vehicle's movement and create a \"accel_vibe_floor\" for x,y and z axis."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-measuring-vibration.rst:69
#: a2fdf861b7aa4a1d8eca79b67a57a6e6
msgid "Calculate the difference between the latest accel values and the accel_vibe_floor."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-measuring-vibration.rst:71
#: b89c3288cdf44a6dbe68ae3ee1b80bd7
msgid "Square the above differences, filter at 2hz and then calculate the square root (for x, y and z).  These final three values are what appear in the VIBE msg's VibeX, Y and Z fields."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-measuring-vibration.rst:75
#: aea3483b934a4e1cae520b5351ffc936
msgid "When looking at vibrations in a log the first thing to look at is the clipping. If the clipping is 0 that’s good. That means the vibrations that are being detected aren’t overwhelming the IMU."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-measuring-vibration.rst:77
#: f29b4ba301434e1aa2988fa2ec8493c2
msgid "When troubleshooting the vibration, consider the axis of the vibration as a staring point to find the problem:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-measuring-vibration.rst:79
#: 582da4e7fd154b519a90b0d1fe297ccb
msgid "If X AND Y are both high, then you may have an issue with a motor bearing or prop balance. Or you may need more/better overall vibration damping for your FC. If X OR Y is high then you may have an issue with your FC mounting. Maybe a wire is bouncing on the FC or restraining it. Or maybe your vibration damping works better in one axis than the other. If you have a Z vibration then you may have a track issue with propeller (bent blade) or vertical play in a motor."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-measuring-vibration.rst:83
#: f7798fa34e1d4a9fbf0135d27370aa01
msgid "Also consider that some flight conditions/airframes will have different natural vibrations. If the vibrations look good in a hover, but they increase with speed, perhaps there is an aerodynamic issue with the airframe, or in wind,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-measuring-vibration.rst:86
#: fac2a27b6a334ac099bb791883af6f42
msgid "Looking for \"The Leans\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-measuring-vibration.rst:88
#: 21e2f996c783489b8fd0bbf762972379
msgid "**The Leans** occurs when the vehicle's attitude estimate becomes incorrect causing it to lean significantly even though the pilot is commanding level flight.  The cause of the problem is often accelerometer aliasing which can be confirmed by comparing the Roll and Pitch attitude estimates from each of the estimation systems (i.e. each AHRSs or EKFs).  The attitude estimates should be within a few degrees of each other"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-measuring-vibration.rst:90
#: 35aa67243f1245fe9895089bc4cea0de
msgid "Download the dataflash log and open in the ground station's log viewer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-measuring-vibration.rst:91
#: ad7b78a1ecc741e88740b05b71ba96c7
msgid "Compare the AHRS2.Roll, NKF1[0].Roll and NKF1[1].Roll if using EKF2, or AHRS2.Roll, XKF1[0].Roll and XKF1[1].Roll, if using EKF3"
msgstr ""

